In article <19970728193401.PAA16859@ladder01.news.aol.com>, beerisfree@aol.com 
(Beerisfree) writes:
|>
|>   Hello, I've been haveing a problem with my notebook. I can't seem to
|>allocate my primary; C:\. When I start my laptop it flashs a warning at me
|>saying 
|>   "Partion is corrupted or does not exist, Save to Disk feature is
|>diabled" 
|>Can you guys please help me out here....? I can't even use my notebook
|>now. 

It does sound as if you've managed to corrupt the disk partitions.  The VP
uses a separate, non-bootable partition (factory set at 42MB) to handle the
save-to-disk function, and it seems the BIOS can't find it, so that's why
you get the error message. But if the C partition is there, it should boot.
Since it doesn't, I suspect you've hosed it as well.

I'm not sure what to suggest at this point.  I don't suppose you were running
Norton Utilities and its DISKIMAGE program, were you?  If you were, you'd
have a hope of recovering the partition.  (You'd use the PHDISK program to
create the save-to-disk partition, as described in the notebook's manual.)

Do you have a bootable floppy you can boot from? (You DID make the backup
floppies when you first got the system, yes?)  If you boot from floppy, can
you change to C:?  If not, you might see if a local computer shop has someone
who knows how to recover corrupted disk partitions.  If all else fails,
you'll need to reformat and re-FDISK the disk then reinstall all the software.
