That would change the host name only.

What about machine name? Yeah, sure, all network commands should work
just fine, but what if something doesn't? When you setup BIND, adding
domains to your host names, the machine name changes also. Now, Digital
UNIX can recognize domain in a host name and it makes no confict. But if
you make the machine name and host name something that doesn't match,
won't it just "drop dead" at some point?

Situations where you tamper with something that is fundamental and
you're not sure of all the implications are *very* susceptable to
Murphy's Law. In other words, you'll find out that it doesn't work when
you load your machine fully and set it up for work.

Well, if this hasn't turned our friend away from this idea, then this is
what you should check:

1. machine name in /etc/rc.config,
2. kernel configuration files at /sys and /sys/config,
3. host name with netsetup or look for config files for INet (I think I
saw it in /etc/rc.config as well),

4. *good luck*.

Nix.
