> Here's my hardware problem for the month...
> 
> I purchased a VAX 6000-210 recently that had a "XMI To DSSI" controller in it
> (a T2036) but the DSSI hard disks and cabinet were removed (only paid $75.00
> for the whole thing).  I recently purchased a mess of RF72 hard disks and
> chassis really cheep and I'm trying to get everything to work.  I have all
> the cables and such.
> 
> First, does this controller have any internal setup that I need to run to
> get it going?  And second, what boot command do I need to execute to get
> it to boot to one of the hard disks?  How many hard disks can this
> controller handle as I have about 15 hard disks that I can stick on this
> thing if it will take it.

There is _some_ setup, but it's used only in special circumstances.  You
probably don't need to do that.

The 6000-210 boot command you use is dependant on which XMI slot the
controller is in.
It's basically something like BOOT/XMI:<slot>/R5:0 <device name>.  There
are other qualifiers, do a SHOW BOOT and see what else is there.  You
can add and delete those boot definitions as well when the key is set to
"update."

DSSI is similar to SCSI, each "bus" can support 7 devices (plus the
controller).
 
> Another thing is, I have a 6000-340, I tried this controller in it and when
> I did a "show configuration" it showed up as "???????", but on the 6000-210
> it shows up right.  Again, is their something I need to do to get this to
> run on that machine.

The 6000-340 might need a firmware upgrade to recognize that device.
 
