> The reason the Alpha 21164PC is so cheap is that it lacks the L2 cache the 21164/21164A has:
> 
> 21164/21164A:  8K+8K I/D L1 Cache + 96K L2 Cache
> 21164PC:       16K+8K I/D L1 Cache only
> 
> The Alpha only runs Windows NT, Digital UNIX, OpenVMS, and Linux.  The Alpha chip CANNOT run Intel x86 binaries!  This
> prevents it from running most games (although it does have DirectX support).  Therefore, for games, they'll have to be
> written specifically for Alpha.  Since NT doesn't allow direct access to hardware, game performance is sluggish compared to
> Windows 95 (except for games that are OpenGL-based like GLQuake).  The Alpha cannot run Windows 95 (which has a heavily
> x86-only kernel).
> 
> Digital, however, has release a "x86 binary compiler" call 'FX!32' -- yes, compiler NOT emulator -- which is 99% effective.
> It's the only product of its kind PERIOD!  After you run an x86 binary once, a BRAND NEW Alpha-binary executable (along with
> associated DLL's) are created.  After a few more runs, the executable is OPTIMIZED (about 2-6 runs) for the Alpha.  In a true
> 21164, the performance is 30-60% of a Pentium MHz for MHz (ex:  Office for Win32/x86 runs like a 200-350MHz Pentium on a
> Alpha 21164-600MHz).  Although its possible that some x86 games will work on Alpha/NT, it's doubtful that it'll replace x86
> systems.
> 
> Many business applications have been ported the Alpha (i.e. released in 21x64 binary form) like Office (and then Office can
> perform like its on a 1GHz Pentium!!!).  With the forthcoming Alpha 21264, Digital is poised increase the performance of the
> Alpha another 60% MHz for MHz (meaning the entry 264-600MHz will perform like a 164-1GHz).  By the time Intel releases
> "Merced" in 1999, the Alpha 364 will be out and posting a SPECfp95 >100!!!  With those benchmarks, you may be seeing games
> with both Intel and Alpha binaries ...
> 
>                               Avail    Core   Cost   SPECint95/fp95
> - Intel RoadMap:
> Intel Pentium ][  300MHz      1997Q3  32-bit  $1800     12       8
> Intel "Deschutes" 400MHz      1998Q2  32-bit   ---      18      12
> Intel "Merced"      ?MHz      1999Q2  64-bit   ---       ?       ?
> - Digital Roadmap:
> Digital Alpha 164 600MHz      Now     64-bit  $2000     18      27
> Digital Alpha 264 600MHz      1998Q1  64-bit   ---      30      50
> Digital Alpha 364   ?MHz      1999Q2  64-bit   ---       ?    >100
> 
> P.S.  If you wonder why the Alpha is so fast -- the reason is simple:  THE DESIGN IS SIMPLE -- i.e. NEARLY TRUE RISC.
> Digital can do MMX-type operations faster than Intel's 57-opcode MMX set in <10 instructions.  The processor is designed to
> do -- ONLY -- 32-bit and 64-bit INSTRUCTIONS!  No 8-bit or 16-bit (and, until recently, couldn't even load 8 or 16-bit
> values).  Digital left out nearly any instruction that could cause a bottleneck or reduce performance.  Intel, on the other
> hand, is going to regret adding 57 new instructions when it's dual-core "Merced" chipset is released (the die is going to be
> huge, and x86 performance will be lower than "Deschutes" @ 400MHz).
> 

