There have been reports of some graphics cards not ID'ing properly in
the Starion systems (I'm assuming that this is a PCI-based card). One
solution has been to load the new BIOS update release located on
Digital's WEB site:

MAIN Site:

http://www.windows.digital.com/

Specific pointer to BIOS:

http://www.windows.digital.com/~ftp/index/ix_Starion_9xx.htm

Filename of BIOS update:

BG9C105.TXT - Instructions for Starion 9XX BIOS v1.04 NEW 5-MAY-97
BG9C105C.EXE - Starion BIOS v1.04 for Starion 950 & 960 Systems ONLY New
5-5-97

The Starion has built-in S3 video which will detect the new video
controller during boot up. It should disable the on-board S3 and use the
new card. Connect the display cable to the new card and power on, if its
working correctly, you should see the video display.

<<Except that win95 device manager still showed the integrated
chipset..which I find unexceptable in my case.>>

As to Windows95 finding the on-board S3 controller, that's really not a
problem. Just go into the Display properties under the Device Manager
and check the "disable in this profile" box. That frees up any resources
that Windows95 might allocate to the device (it really is disabled in
BIOS). While it may show up in Device Manager, it's disabled and not
being used and doesn't cause any problems (except from an aesthetic
viewpoint).

regards,

glen kelley
