Well, it happened, but I'm not sure what the fault is. The trader in question
also posted his results to the RISKS mailing list (http://catless.ncl.ac.uk/Risks);
here's an extract from Volume 19, #24:

|So, I wrote a stand alone program.  Sure enough, the machine with the
|latest rev motherboard (one that was just replaced by DEC) is
|producing bad numbers.  Time to try 'dxcalc', DEX's X calculator.
|Yup. different numbers.  How about perl?  Yup, different numbers.  How
|about 'bc'?  Duh, bc doesn't take floating point powers.  Hmm... check
|libm.  Nope, they are the same.
|
|Bottom line:  DEC will be here shortly.

and in the next issue (#25):

|So, as it turns out, DEC finally made it to our site and replaced the
|motherboard.  Everything worked fine after that.  Unfortunately, I was on
|vacation at the time and missed the service call (and the opportunity to
|compare boards / chip batch numbers).  I'm kind of bummed about that, but
|alas, that's life.
|
|DEC claimed it was a bad chip on *that* board only.

I suppose the chip could have somehow made it past the standard testing done 
after fabrication, or could have failed later on. I haven't heard of anyone
reproducing the problem (yet?).

