The act of failing, whether while powered or not, whether while is use or
storage or just after or during manufacture or later in life is all
statistically probable.

There is a graph called the bathtub curve, as it resembles a bathtub, that
shows that most failure occur early on in life and later on in life,
whether a reasonably steady state of failure in between the two extremes.
This steady state of failure is quite low, given that nobody has been
inside messing around with things. This lifetime curve is in fact
reflected in a great deal of products, not just computers. Cars, toasters,
blenders, VCR's, ...

So, the neat thing about this curve, is that when you work out the math
based on observation and then extrapolation, and if you get really wild
and throw in all the variables like the lifetime of each inidividual chip,
capacitor, resistor, or even go further to the level of each individual
gate in each chip, well, the neat thing is, failures are predicted while
stuff is just sitting there, doing nothing. Observations by myself within
manufacturing bear this truth out. In the days when a manufacturer had 60
or 120 days of inventory on hand, a cetain amount of that raw material
coming to the production floor was defective, simply had reached the end
of it's life by sitting on a shelf. In these days where inventory is
managed as per Just In Time and there may be no more than 1 day or even 4
hours of inventory on hand, well, the incidence of defective material is
quite low. This is because it is not very far along the bathtub curve of
life.

Jeez, now that I think of it, humans fit the bathtub, quite well too ;-)

Regards, Richard

