Philip Hawthorne wrote:
> 
> I wonder can someone help me?
> 
> I have an old, working LA210 wide carriage printer with  a serial interface
> which I want to use for logging purposes. For use with a PC, the manual
> (and other documentation found at Digital's Web Site) talk about adding the
> line "xon/xoff" to autoexec.bat since the printer uses xon/xoff protocol
> flow control.
> 
> I have vague memory of using this command once before. Our visiting Digital
> engineer also remembers seeing "xon/xoff" in th past but can't recall
> where.
> 
> It seems a strange - not to say invalid - name for a DOS program?
> 
> Anybody heard tell of it, have it or can help me control the printer any
> other way? Without it the printer stalls after a few hundred characters.

Digital supplied such a program XONXOF.EXE (XONXOFF.EXE) with earlier
OEM distributions of MS-DOS with Digital PC's (it started with the
VAXmate and its MS-DOS 3.01 release).

It was also for a long time part of the PATHWORKS for DOS kit (or
VAX/VMS Services for PCs a.k.a. PCSA as it was know before the
name "PATHWORKS").

You might still be able to find it in 

    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/PCI

(check the index file there).

I also know that it is in the libraries of the CompuServe forum
DECPC (there are also other similar utilities such as LPTCOM,
LPTCOM1, LPTCOM2, etc., there).

You might also want to search archives such as 

    http://www.shareware.com

for XONXOF*, LPTCOM* (especially if you don't have CompuServe
access, or you can't find it on sites as those mentioned
above).

You can also try an advanced query with suitable terms
through

   http://altavista.digital.com/cgi-bin/query?pg=aq

In any case, the purpose of DOS TSRs (Terminate and Stay
Resident) utilities such as these was to handle the 
XON/XOFF handshaking for serial ports.

