Mike Mitchell (mitchell@godot.radonc.unc.edu) wrote:
:      Folks-
: 
:      I have an old VAXstation II/GPX that has recently lost
: the ability to keep good time.  It now loses several minutes
: a day.  I'm assuming that there is a battery back-up clock chip
: on the thing somewhere.  I pulled all the boards and looked for
: an appropriate looking chip or battery, but just can't find it.
: I'm familiar with the battery back-up chips on more modern
: machines.  Does anyone know whether this old beast has a battery
: or clock chip somewhere that needs changing and where I might start
: looking for it?  For all I know it is under the bottom of the
: chassis!
: 
As another reply mentions, there's a NiCad battery on the front cover that
connects to the cpu module.  This is the battery that runs the TOY clock and
keeps the boot parameter memory alive.

However, if your system is losing time while the operating system is running,
then the TOY clock is not your problem.  Even if the battery were dead, it'd
ask you for the time when it boots (if VMS, don't recall what Ultrix does).
If you're losing time, then you probably have something keeping the machine at
a high IPL level, locking out clock interrupts.  Check for any new devices,
device drivers added/modified lately.  Also, check for high memory error rates
(since they are handled at IPL31) or cpu machine checks.

        Stu



