In article <33CCB082.4EAE@zko.dec.com>, John L. Henning wrote:
>Running them is not the point.  Running them 
>  - at full speed 
>  - with non-heroic tuning 
>  - with mainstream compilers
>  - with the windowing system and network system up
>  - on both Alpha & Intel
>is the point.

We could argue about how meaningful the SPEC benchmark suite is for typical
applications, but that's been done often enough already. But, consider this:

- how does a workload compare on Unix vs. NT when it starts paging?
- how does it compare if several other applications "typical" for each
  OS run at the same time? On Unix this would probably be X clients and
  perhaps an X server of some sort, with NT it would be its windowing system
  and several graphical toys which usually hang around on the desktop.
  (in case of workstations)

What most of us probably learned is that there are extremely well-written
C and Fortran compilers available for NT now, which require little tuning
for best results, which is a Good Thing.

On the other hand, at least in my case, even 20% speed difference in typical
compiler output would have little influence on the decision about which
operating system to use. A cost difference of >$1000 weighs much more,
especially considering the low cost of the relatively powerful workstations
available these days.

-nino
