Has anyone been successful in getting Visual C++ 2.0 to run under NT 4.0
on an Alpha?  I'm taking a Dr Watson error in MSVC.EXE when I exit an
App through Visual C's development environment. From the error message,
it appears MSVC.EXE or some DLL is attempting to write to virtual
address 0x0.  On closer inspection using WINDBG, it looks like the
processor register used to form the address is getting loaded with zero
instead of the correct value. The App exits *without* error when run
from the Explorer or when the compiler is run under WINDBG.  It also
exits w/o err when run from VC++ 2.0 under NT 3.51.

Installing VC++ 2.0 into NT 4.0 didn't go without a hitch. During the
installation, setup kept complaining about not being able to open
various MSVC*.DLLs because they were read-only or opened by some other
app. I found killing the explorer got around this problem. The
installation continued normally until it neared the end where it popped
up a message complaining that it could not write to the registry.  I was
not able to resolve this problem. I examined the registry and it looked
correct so I decided to ignore the message and tried using the
compiler.  

Installing SP3 for NT40 doesn't correct the problem. Nor does installing
VC++ 2.0 onto NT 3.51 and upgrading to NT 4.0. I tried the upgrade to
see if the error had anything to do with problems I experienced during
the direct installation of VC++ 2.0 onto NT 4.0.



