mj,

The hard drive on the 486 is undoubtedly jumpered in the "neutral"
position, used when it is the only IDE drive connected to the IDE
interface.  Find out what brand it is, connect to the drive mfr's web
site, and you'll find the jumper settings.  Set up the hard drive as
"master", the CD-ROM as "slave", and leave the CD-ROM drive (Drive 1)
as "Not installed" in the 486 CMOS setup.  Install the IDE CD-ROM
driver in CONFIG.SYS and MSCDEX in AUTOEXEC.BAT.  Or pray that Windows
Plug-and-Pray finds it... Ben Myers

