In comp.os.minix Mark Holcroft <mwh@sasib.co.uk> wrote:

: I demonstrated a method that could have applied using as little as a
: 5bit character set to extend the range of years expressible by 2
: characters well past 2000. 

That was pretty cool. But to take it further, why not a simple *one*-byte
(8-bit) *binary* number? Base it on 1900, and you're good until 2155 (or
2027, if you make it a signed number). I realize this wouldn't be
applicable in all situations, but similar solutions (to one or the other
of these approaches) should always be available. Decimal 99 only takes up
7 bits, with room for 28 numbers left over. And it's precisely those
leftovers that make the Y2K problem seem so silly. Computers don't count
in decimal... well, aside from BCD... which I suppose is just what the
afflicted programs are using. (?)

