<HTML>
Philip Hawthorne wrote:
<BLOCKQUOTE TYPE=CITE>I wonder can someone help me?

<P>I have an old, working LA210 wide carriage printer with&nbsp; a serial
interface
<BR>which I want to use for logging purposes. For use with a PC, the manual
<BR>(and other documentation found at Digital's Web Site) talk about adding
the
<BR>line "xon/xoff" to autoexec.bat since the printer uses xon/xoff protocol
<BR>flow control.

<P>I have vague memory of using this command once before. Our visiting
Digital
<BR>engineer also remembers seeing "xon/xoff" in th past but can't recall
<BR>where.

<P>It seems a strange - not to say invalid - name for a DOS program?

<P>Anybody heard tell of it, have it or can help me control the printer
any
<BR>other way? Without it the printer stalls after a few hundred characters.

<P>TIA

<P>Philip H.</BLOCKQUOTE>
XON/XOFF is not a program of any kind.&nbsp; It is a flow control protocol
built into the serial driver.&nbsp; XON and XOFF are common names for the
ASCII codes DC1 and DC3 (device controls 1 and 3).&nbsp; Whne using XON/XOFF,
the computer may send at a faster rate than the printer can print.&nbsp;
Thus the printers buffer fills up.&nbsp; At some point, usually around
90%, the printer sends XOFF and the computer stops sending for a while.&nbsp;
When the printer buffer falls below, say 50%, the printer sends XON and
the computer responds by sending some more.

<P>You can select XON/XOFF (software) flow control or RTS/CTS (hardware
flow control) by setting up the properties for the serial port, or by a
command to the comm driver, or in some applications by configuration of
the app which then controls the behavior of the comm port.

<P>Again, it is not a program.

<P>Les
<BR>&nbsp;</HTML>




