In article <5quk9e$6i6$2@news.bayarea.net>, bdynin@movil.com (Boris Dynin) wrote:
>By the way, does anybody know what Galaxies is?  It's supposed to be yet 
>unannounced DEC software.  But what does it do?
>

I'm not all the way (or even part of the way) up to date on this - but the 
"Galaxy" technology/product being produced by Digital is targetted at 
Digital's VMS operating system and is the next generation in 
multi-node/multi-processor computing for VMS.

Apparently, you can schedule CPU, and memory resources using Galaxy such that 
physically distinct nodes appear to be a very large virtual multi-processor 
machine (with very high performance). Likewise, processors within a single 
multi-processor machine can be configured to function as individual 
systems/nodes for high-availability, fault resistent type configurations.

You might check out Digital's web site and comp.os.vms for more information, 
particularly since the above is really just a very weak understanding.

drew

