John L. Henning <henning@zko.dec.com> wrote:
>Running them is not the point.  Running them
>  - at full speed
>  - with non-heroic tuning
>  - with mainstream compilers
>  - with the windowing system and network system up
>  - on both Alpha & Intel
>is the point.


Jon Leech <jon@cs.unc.edu> writes:
>    So is it fair to conclude that DEC's Unix compiler group is less
>competent at tuning defaults for SPEC95 than DEC's (or is it Microsoft's) NT
>compiler group?
>    If so, I don't understand why this is something DEC would want to make a
>point of.

Here's my take...

The claim made is that if you don't go for speed, then WinNT is not
much slower than Unix on equal hardware.  Perhaps we are supposed to
actually believe the following:

           In order to emphasize CPU performance for mainstream users
           who are not interested in heroic tuning, Digital chose to submit
           base results only, using Windows NT with Visual Fortran and
           Visual C++.

One is tempted to ask why they chose to submit "peak" results 130 times
for Unix, then?  Of course when you go for speed by using different
compiler switches the Unix numbers jump up.  But presumably the NT
peak numbers are lousy since Microsoft's front end doesn't collect the
information that DEC's back end needs to optimize properly.

Reading between the lines, it seems like a lot of fluster to hide
a 15% slowdown on NT by emphasizing a 5% slowdown on "base" numbers
instead.

