Robert Harley wrote:

> Perhaps we are supposed to actually believe the following:
> 
>      In order to emphasize CPU performance for mainstream users
>      who are not interested in heroic tuning, Digital chose to submit
>      base results only, using Windows NT with Visual Fortran and
>      Visual C++.

Well, I believe it.  I had a rough mental outline for the white 
paper prior to starting the measurements and the above is an
accurate statement of my intent at the time. 

It is ALSO true that there are more opportunities to go be a hero 
under Unix.  There's a bigger selection of switches, tools, and
feedback mechanisms.  (The white paper makes a point of this in
the section titled "The Value of Feedback".)  

> One is tempted to ask why they chose to submit "peak" results 
> 130 times for Unix, then?  

Three reasons.
   1. People tend to quote only peak. 
   2. The peak numbers are better, due to additional mechanisms 
      being available.
   3. In a Unix culture, switch-twiddling may be more likely
      than in a point-and-click culture.

> But presumably the NT peak numbers are lousy 

Not exactly lousy.  Bear in mind that peak is always greater than or
equal to base.  Therefore we know that on NT a peak submission of the
600a would be at minimum 16.3 int and 18.4 fp. That would be #2 in 
the handy integer summary table at IDEAS
  http://www.ideasinternational.com/benchmark/spec/specint.html
and just barely in the top 10 for FP if you create your own 
summary by restricting the search to single-cpu systems with 
SPEC's advanced query tool
  http://www.specbench.org/cgi-bin/osgresults?conf=cfp95;op=form 

> since Microsoft's front end doesn't collect the
> information that DEC's back end needs to optimize properly.

For DIGITAL Visual Fortran, Digital is responsible for both front
and back.  For Visual C++, you are correct that Microsoft has the
front end and Digital the back end.  It is also true that some 
optimizations are missed as a result of looser integration between
front and back, but I think the numbers show that the optimization
which *is* done is nevertheless both proper and respectable.

> Reading between the lines, it seems like a lot of fluster 

Thank you

> to hide a 15% slowdown on NT by emphasizing a 5% slowdown 
> on "base" numbers instead.

Please compare base to base.  The range was 88% to 102%.

For those who prefer a peak to peak comparison, sure, we'll do NT
peak at some point.  But the more I have learned about peak tuning,
the more I think that it is in the interest of all vendors and in
the interest of all users if vendors do a better job of moving 
their best high-performance tweaks into the defaults, or into
more easily understood "wholesale" switches.  We should put more
emphasis on base.

/John Henning
 Speaking for myself, not Digital



