On 17 Jul 1997 07:53:46 GMT A.Arnold (arnold@zam331.kfa-juelich.de) wrote:
>
>>I just got an Alpha Server 300 (4/266) w/32 Mb RAM. It came with
>>Digital Unix 4.0 installed at the factory.  After booting it up
>>for the first time, I answered the install questions (hostname,
>>password, security, etc), then it rebuilt the kernel and rebooted.
>>
>>The systems seems extraordinarily slow, as in when I log in to CDE,
>>it takes about 5 minutes for windows to come up.  Really.
>>Starting a new DECTerm takes about 30 seconds.  Running just about
>>any command makes the drives whir like crazy.
>>
>>I'm not a system administrator (and I don't play one on TV)
>>but I didn't think I answered any of the install questions
>>so wrongly.  Perhaps the factory settings for performance
>>just aren't good at all ?  Is 32Megs not enough ?

32MB is definitely at the low end, but you can get it working much
better than it is at the moment.  The reason it is slow is because
it's paging stuff in and out of memory onto disk all the time.

We got a 255/230 with 32MB.  And it ran like a dog, much as you 
describe.  The first thing I did was to remove the CDE (I mean,
configure it to use xdm instead), which made
things quite a lot better.  The next thing is to rebuild the 
kernel as small as possible - if you use a default one, or
include all the options that you might possibly want it can be
quite bloated, but if you configure it carefully it can get much
smaller.  Unless I was missing something this is a bit of a 
shot-in-the-dark kind of job though.  
The one I've currently got is just under 8MB (this 
requires some spiffy graphics stuff) - probably I could get it
smaller if I tried.  It might also be a good idea to strip it,
unless you need the symbols for kernel debugging.

The best solution of course is to buy more memory though.

