Jon Leech wrote:
>     So is it fair to conclude that DEC's Unix compiler group is 
> less competent at tuning defaults for SPEC95 than DEC's (or is 
> it Microsoft's) NT compiler group?

Nothing I write should be taken as casting aspersions on any 
engineer.  Different groups and different companies work to 
different business goals and different sets of user expectations.

Besides, with performance ranging from 88% to 102%, one should not 
read this as either one being better; one should read this as both 
performing in very similar fashion.  

>    [...] I don't understand why this is something DEC would want 
> to make a  point of.

From a business point of view: if you're used to Unix 
workstations, and you are considering buying an NT workstation, 
here's a way to look at them *under* *NT* but using
a metric that you are familiar with from Unix workstations.

From a personal point of view: I started using NT seriously in 
approximately February.  After an initial learning curve, and 
after installing the NT Resource Kit which thankfully gives my
fingers the two- and three-letter Unix commands that I cannot 
unlearn, I have been assimilated^H^H^H^H^H^H^H^H^H^H^H^H I mean
I have found that I like it.

From a performance point of view: If your sole criterion is the 
flat-out best possible performance, pick Unix.  If you like 
exploring compiler switches and profiling tools and link-time
optimizers, Unix is currently more mature than NT.  But if 
performance is ONE criterion among several criteria, and if 
you do not engage in heroic tuning, then you may find that 
your other criteria evidence more significant differences for 
Unix vs. NT than the relatively small differences seen on the 
performance criterion (+2% to -12%).

/John Henning
