In article <5qgipn$7q1$1@chinet.chinet.com>, edlee@chinet.chinet.com (Edward Lee) writes:
:...I would like to suggest a change to a code sample in the Alpha AXP 
:Architecture Reference Manual, 2nd edition, in section 4.6.1...
:...The original code will work most of the time, but it will fail in certain 
:situations where the two strings are identical...
:...I hope that someone at Digital gets this message to the appropriate people 
:to change the books and change any libraries which may have used the book's 
:original code example for comparing two strings.

  The following response is a quote from one of the folks responsible
  for the Alpha Architecture Reference Manual (ARM):

"Thanks for your comment.  The example in the ARM is intended to
demonstrate possible uses of the CMPBGE instruction, and should
not be considered a complete routine for comparing character
strings.  As you point out, the example provides no way to indicate
the length of the strings to be compared.

Assuming one wants to compare NUL-terminated strings, as in C's
strcmp routine, your suggested replacement code would work.  Note
that if control reaches your "NOTEQUAL" label, the strings might
still be equal up to the terminating NUL in each string.

We may change the ARM example to something like the following:


To compare two null-terminated strings for greater/equal/less:

	<initialize R1 to aligned QW address of string1>
	<initialize R2 to aligned QW address of string2>
LOOP:
	LDQ	R3, 0(R1)		; Pick up 8 bytes of string1
	LDA	R1, 8(R1)		; Increment string1 pointer
	LDQ	R4, 0(R2)		; Pick up 8 bytes of string2
	LDA	R2, 8(R2)		; Incremente string2 pointer
	CMPBGE	R31, R3, R6		; Test for zeros in string1
	XOR	R3, R4, R5		; Test for all equal bytes
	BNE	R6, DONE		; Exit if a zero found
	BEQ	R5, LOOP		; Else repeat if all equal
DONE:	CMPBGE	R31, R5, R5		;
	...
; At this point, R5 can be used to determine the first not-equal
; byte position (if any), and R6 can be used to determine the
; position of the terminating zero in string 1 (if any).

By the way, none of Digital's library string-compare routines run off
the end of a string onto a new page.  (Some routines may fetch beyond
the point of first difference, for better performance.)"


  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
