> > be some kind of a hardware failure (but I'm not sure).  The
> > monitor itself is a bit flaky, but restarting doesn't seem to
> > get that far.  Upon power on the single digit LED indicator
> > on the back of the unit goes through a series of numbers:
> > F8686C-5-8-578---C6 (where the hyphens indicate numbers that
> > went by too fast to see).  Then it stays on the 6. The monitor
> > never does anything...no beep or spewing out of numbers related
> > to startup self tests (32...31...30..etc).
> > Please give us any useful information or ideas how to proceed. Thanks.

I don't know whether or not the 3200 uses the same error codes as the
MicroVAX II, but since it uses the same cabinet kit, it might.

Here are the MVII codes.  They may or may not be useful for your
machine.

F - Waiting for DC OK
E - Waiting for P OK
D - Running checksum test on ROM
C - Searching for RAM memory required for ROM programs
B - Read KA630-An IPCR register (accesses Q22-Bus)
A - Testing VCB01 video console display (if present)
9 - Identifying console terminal
8 - Language inquiry or CPU halted
7 - Running data tests on RAM memory
6 - Running address tests on RAM memory
5 - Running tests that use Q22 bus map to access local memory
4 - CPU instruction and register tests
3 - Running interrupt tests
2 = Searching for bootstrap device
1 - Bootstrap device found
0 - Testing completed

I would recommend removing your video controller board(s) and attaching
a terminal to the console port.  Unless you move the remaining boards
over to fill the gap left by removing the video boards, your system
won't boot, but you will be able to run the diagnostics.

Good luck!

