These days, one often sees old VAX equipment and peripherals given away
or sold very cheaply.  Quite apart from the nostalgia aspect, in some
cases such free hardware can be a good bargain.  I'm on the lookout for
a VAX (see my recent post) and often tape drives etc come as part of the
bargain; I would like to give some of this old equipment a good home.

In some cases, I might prefer to connect a tape drive directly to my
ALPHA.  This doesn't always work; some tape drives which work on VAXes
work fine when connected to my ALPHA, some don't.  Is there any simple
(or not so simple) rule about what works and what doesn't?  (I'm talking
mostly about third party tape drives which, however, were specifically
marketed to work with VAX and VMS.)  In particular, are there any
parameters I can set on the ALPHA which might change things?  (I've only
tried PKA0_FAST at boot time, which doesn't seem to make any
difference.)  The most common problem is `fatal drive error'; the device 
is, however, visible and the ALPHA can communicate with it (at least 
cause it to make noises and switch indicator lights on and off).  By the 
way, I'm only talking about SCSI stuff, of course.


