In article <33cc8b98.3263533@news.ais.net>, xdevnull@ais.net (/dev/null) writes:
>I recetnly received the Open VMS CDROM from www.montagar.com.  I
>intend to use it to install VMS on my VaxStation 3100/30. 
..
>There is absolutely no documentation with this CDROM, and nothing
>available from their web site. ...

First, if at all possible, get a copy of the Upgrade and Installation Manual,
and the Release Notes for the version of VMS that is on the CD.

The manual will explain that you need to restore the saveset VMSxxx.B from
the top level directory on the CD to disk that will become your new system disk.
("xxx" is the version of OpenVMS -- e.g 062, 071, etc.)

If this is a recent version of OpenVMS VAX, you can either boot the CD as 
you did to get Standalone Backup, or you can boot into SYS1 to get a full
version of VMS booted. (Booting into SYS1 requires something like /R5:10000000
as part of the boot command.)

Once you have done an image backup to restore savset B, reboot the system from 
the disk to which you restored the saveset.  Then follow the instructions that
will be displayed.  This is the point where you might want the manuals -- 
they explain the various questions that you must answer.  You should be able
to get a usable system by following the on-line instructions from the
installation process, but the manuals can be a big help.

