patteeuw@sys2.ped.pto.ford.com (Jack Patteeuw) writes:

> Basem Moussa <moussa@fusebox.com> writes:
><snip>

>>Also, does Digital unix support NFS file-locking?

>Digital UNIX supports the "standard" NFS advisory file locking using lockd
>and statd.  Note I said "advisory".  Digital UNIX does have the capability
>of mandatory file locking, but only on files that are local to a system.

It is the general sense of the NFS community that mandatory lock is the
work of the devil (and not the BSD mascot!)  Even the Posix spec make it
clear that one does not need mandatory locking to be Posix compliant.
I think there are some NFS implementations that support mandatory locking,
but I can't believe they run too well.  Blocking NFS server threads is
a great way to hang a server.

If you expect to be doing locking in a lossy environemnt, there's a patched
rpc.lockd that fixed many problems available from customer support.

That's about the only significant NFS issue we have.  We also support
NFS V3 (the first vendor to ship it by a year or two) and NFS over TCP.
