Quoted from the same article:
"One reviewer of this article kindly pointed out that the SPEC CPU suites are intended
to measure the processor, its memory system, and the compiler. The CPU95 suites do not 
contain significant calls to Operating System services, and in fact sometimes purposely 
avoid the OS. Therefore it is not surprising that a port of compiler technology from 
Unix to NT would result in similar performance under NT. 

This author agrees that the desired contribution by an OS to SPEC CPU95 performance is 
nil, but suggests that this is not always achieved in practice.  It sometimes happens 
that a program will run more slowly than expected on a particular OS or version of an 
OS; for example, a January 1996 field test version of an (unnamed) operating system 
was observed to unintentionally dedicate 10% of the CPU to a network daemon, resulting 
in slower than expected CPU95 performance.

The fact that excellent performance was achieved by compute-intensive benchmarks running 
under Microsoft Windows NT is an indication of NT maturity - it does not get in the way 
of compute-intensive performance."

In other words, NT is not so screwed up as to slowdown programs that do not require 
its services. The same could have been said of good old DOS if equivalent compilers 
have been made available. Progress, oh well...

								Yannis



