If you're worried about reliability, both Alpha and Intel servers tend
to support fast backup devices, RAID arrays, power supplies, and so forth.
If you've decided on running NT, the Alpha's advantage will be its speed,
and possibly scalability (you can switch over to UNIX or OpenVMS if you
have a supported workstation or server, and grow beyond what NT can handle).

> Anybody have any good reccomendations for the best reading material or
> ways to approach a self-taught education on this thing? I saw somebody
> advertising 166Mhz Alpha motherboards a while back for $129, and thought
> this might be a good opportunity to tinker and learn - but I don't even
> know enough to know if this was the right kind of board for what I want ;>.

What do you want? For running a business, you'll have trouble getting support
for a machine you cobble together yourself. For a learning experience, why not?
Note that 600MHz chips are out now, so the board in question is probably priced 
about right.

> I searched for the faq at RTFM - and it looked like it was just on UNIX.
> The best thing would be a 'DEC for Dummies' book - but I'm not holding my
> breath on that one. I would appreciate any pointers to pages, books,
> faqs, that target an audience familiar with computing and networking in
> general, but clueless as to DEC/Alpha specifics

In general, you shouldn't have to worry about the CPU under the hood - a server
is a server. There is quite a bit of documentation on products and the Alpha
chip specifically at www.digital.com, if you haven't been there yet.

Good luck!



