Stuart Brook <sbrook@concentric.net> writes:

>Assuming the patches come from Digital or Mentec ... but privately
>produced patches would have to be produced by someone misusing a sources
>license, or someone who is reverse engineering the code.

Not necessarily... since stripped sources come with each binary
kit.

One of the hallmarks of the pdp-11 community used to be the
sharing of information.  It wasn't unheard of for people to
develop changes to the uncommented sources and produce a
set of .DIF files which would implement their changes.  This
way, they could make them freely available.  Only someone with
the exact same set of sources from the binary kit would then
be able to apply them.  Sometimes, such changes were even
published in newsletters devoted to RT-11 (anyone else miss the
Mini-Tasker?)

A perfect example of this was the set of changes which implemented
RX03 support in the DY handler.  It was not developed by Digital.
It was developed by a private individual who made his changes
available for people to use or not as they saw fit.

Same thing for files which are distributed only in binary form.  If
a person were to make changes in, let's say, a utility, using the
sources which they have on a legal source kit, and if they were to
produce a .DIF for the changes, only someone with a BINARY kit for
the same version would be able to apply the changes (if they chose
to).  I *can* see that there would be a problem if someone made the
entire binary file available.. since then someone without a license
for that software would be able to get something for free...

So long as a person doesn't represent themselves as providing
patches sanctioned by Digital or Mentec, and so long as the person
does not charge in any way for the changes, and so long as the people
who apply the changes understand that they cannot get support for
a system which includes the changes, I can't see that there
would be any problem (there didn't used to be).  People would still
have to come to Mentec or Digital for anything official and supported.

As a long-time developer of RT-11, I knew that there were many
customers and users of RT who were easily as knowledgable and
capable of finding and fixing problems as *any* in the actual
development team.  Sometimes the best ideas came from the field.

					Megan Gentry
