A very nice system indeed. If the serial number starts with KA I built it
Kanata, Canada and if it starts with AY the a friend of mine built it in
Ayr.

DECwindows, aka, X-Motif, is a Windowing system created in conjunction
with a huge bunch of industry players and MIT. It is just like Windows,
conceptually. It is not technically similar at all. You must have the
ethernet connection terminated properly, either the AUI port or the
10base2, ThinWire port. DECnet does not like running without terminiation
and in fatc, the Lance (Network Interface Chip) would either hang up
OpenVMS during boot or would cause the OS to crash after a short run-time
if the network was not terminiated.

All OpenVMS systems were reccomended to be installed with DECwindows, even
though they did not have display hardware directly. You see, Microsoft
would have called this a server, but in Motif parlance, this is the
client. The client runs the application, the Server rpovides the display,
keyboard and mouse. Thus, any PC running a Motif capable Server, such as
eXcursion can make use of DECwindows on this system via the network.

Now for a bit of, we did it right, but Microsoft sold ir better.

You see, an application such as DECwrite ran on the client, the VAXserver
3100. As the program made extensive use of shared libraries, and as the OS
is OPENVMS VAX and it is designed to use what is loaded in memory and not
make individual copis of an application in memory for each instance, it is
better to use DECwindows. For example, 10 people running DECwrite are all
using the same single copy in memory. 10 poeple running Word are all
running each a single copy of Word for a total of 10 copies of word. If a
new version of DECwrite comes out, everyone gets it at the same time and
it is installed only once. With the Word scenario, well, you have to
coordinate 10 people updating each of their own copies of word. I couild
go on and on, and I would, but my temperature is rising right about now.
If the Microsoft way is just starting to sound even remotely inefficient
to you, then you are an astute reader. Suffice it to say,

"The prime mandate of an company, is to return a profit to it's
investors." Microsoft does this very, very well. They do not design very
good Operating Systems, their technology is a moving target, subject to
the whims of marketing from day to day, their quality is almost laughingly
abysmal, and the grunt at the top hailed as a technology whizbang, is not,
he is a financial whizbang. Bill Gates wouldn't know sound, effecient and
affordable technology if it hit him over the head.

Regards, Richard

