It was designed as an entry level workstation for Unix, not NT & to 
compete with (at the time) low-end SUN systems.

Regarding the CPU speed, all processors of the same design & process 
will come off the production line at some stage & be binned according 
to the spped that they reach in tests. It's a fact that the 
semiconductor processes yiled a variance of performance across a wafer 
& some CPU's will end up at 100Mhz, others (from the same wafer) might 
pass tests at 233Mhz or faster. There will be  e centre point to the 
yield that increases as the process improves, but tthere will always be 
runts of the litter - your 100Mhz machine is, unfortunately, there.

You might find however that you can upgrade quite easily as the 
Motherboards will probably be common, & now the 21064A is obsolete, 
replacement CPU's quite cheap. I think that you will be able to go up 
to 233 as a minimum & possibly faster.

Jim





