Hi,
I think I have found a bug in AdvFS that effects perfomance of the File
System that reduces the draw of a file system from 9.5MB/sec to 3MB/sec
on HSZ and KZP-SC controllers.
The test is very very simple if you have the space!!

Please note the hardware config at the end of this note!

1) Create a large file, I used a 1.2GB file.
2) Using the utility of your choice ( I used dd ) read the file in
blocks of 8K or bigger writing it to /dev/null if you have no space or
to another file system if you do!
Using a performance monitoring tool of choice ( I used pmgr ) note the
transfer rate and transaction rate. I got 8 - 9.5 MB/sec at 
150 transactions/sec

At this stage all should be fine
3) Write a very simple C program that opens and reads the file in the
same block size as the utility you used. Somewhere in your loop after X
iterations perform a seek. If you seek 1byte or 1million bytes the
result is the same the performance monitor show the transactions rate go
from 150 to 400 and the transfer rate gos from 8 to 3MB/sec. However, if
you seek on the 8k boundary (ie seek 8192 bytes or a multiple threre of)
nothing goes wrong.

I have told DEC Support of this however, they suggest that I start
fiddling with the blocking factor of the controller.....!
Has anyone else noticed this, or has anyone found a way around it, as
there must be people out there using RAID controllers, using AdvFS and
seeking..!

Thanks in Advance

Config Note!
I have tried this out on the folowing :
DEC Alpha 1000, 2100, 4100 8400 Servers running
DEC Unix 3.2c/d/f and 4.0a/b using
RAID controllers KZP-SC or HSZ-50 configured with
RAID 0 or 0+1 or 5 stripes of 2 disks or more
all systems exhibit the same problem!
N.B 1) However, I did try this test on a workstation using its internal
controller and could not replicate the problem, therefore, could this be
the queing with AdvFS and RAID controllers ?
N.B 2) Using the same hardware config I don't get this problem with UFS.
Richard
