In article <5q05v6$831@netnews.upenn.edu>, pgordon@blue.seas.upenn.edu (Peter Gordon) writes:

>I am hoping someone out there can give me some advice as to 
>how to get my DEC 3000 to communicate with a recently acquired
>tape drive, a TLZ04. After plugging the tape drive in, the machine
>(running OSF) looks like it is not using the right device name
>for the tape drive. Can anyone give me some idea on how to get
>this to work? Thanks in advance - 

1.  Select a unique SCSI ID.
2.  Connect to bus.  If you have to unterminate the bus to add
    the drive, shutdown.  If you don't shutdown and connecting
    drive corrupts your data, don't say you weren't warned.
3.  Verify console can see the device.  For built-in busses
    on the 3000 family a "show device" should be sufficient.
    If on a TURBOchannel expansion, use "t tc# cnfg", there
    # is the TURBOchannel number of the extra adapter.
4.  Boot system.  Watch messages to see if the device is
    found.  Verify name against expected; tz#, where # is
    (bus# * 8) + target-id.
5.  If you didn't need to shutdown to add the device, use
    scu(8) to scan the bus:

	# scu scan edt bus #
	# scu show edt bus #

    For the last '#' in the examples, use the bus#.  Look for
    a "Sequential Access" device at the appropriate target ID.

6.  Create the special files:

	# cd /dev
	# ./MAKEDEV tz#

7.  Use tape.

If you have reason to believe that you correctly did all these
steps, please offer a clue what is wrong.
--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




