IE1 Joachim Mueller wrote:
> I have just picked up a MicroVAX II, but unfortunately without
> any harddisks. Now I have some questions:
> 
> In the two cabinets are after removing the frontpanel some connectors
> for harddisks. Which type of harddisk do I have to use?
> (Perhaps ESDI). And which sort of controller should I have to choose
> for the disks?. There is only one quad controller for some big
> harddisks, originally shipped with this machine.


The big hard disks of the RA class were driven by a KDA50 controller. 
An RA82 would take up about 15 amps of power for 600 meg. Not the type
of disk you want running all the time in a house. The KDA50 was
connected to the drives with some big black cables. 

On the MicrovaxII in the big cabinet, you have 2 inner cabinets. Each
has some plugs for connectors in the front cavity, but their presence
does not indicate that there is a controller for such disks.

The KDA50 would not use these front cavities.

These cavities were designed for RD class drives and TK class tape
devices. All "proprietary" Digital technologies. The controller for the 
RD disk drives was an RQDX3 if I remember well.

In essence, if you have a KDA50, hang it on the wall. You might wish to
look for a SCSI controller from CMD or DILOG and then get moderns SCSI
disks. At least the power supply-plugs on the MVII are industry
standard!

> I think they were RA60 or something else. On the system the are called
> DUB0 and DUB1. Can anybody help me?
