In article <5puls9$1be@dilbert.tdg.mtu.edu>, swlaemmr@mtu.edu (Shawn Laemmrich) writes:
>I recently picked up a DEC infoserver 100 from an auction..... I'm
>wondering if anyone can tell me a little about this box, and what I might
>be able to use it with, or point me to some info.  I got just the box (it
>has a HD, some memort, and a cd-rom, nothing else).  I'm wondering if I can
>hook it up using the serial port as the console -- or do anything else with
>it for that matter

Not much from the sound of things. It requires a DEC transport (LAT) in order
to communicate on a network.  It's basically a CDROM server with, probably, an
internal SCSI card for connecting up to seven devices to.  I used to use one on
a DECNET driven network to add CDROM's and a hard disk to my PC - I also served
three library PC's with the CD's.

In short you need LAT which is a DEC product.  You could try and pick up a
microvax and use that to drive a system.

A bit off topic so I'll stop now.

Alastair.



