Distribution: world
References: <01bc8885$6ce1da60$96c95882@aca.mcc.ac.uk> <33be5023.89213835@news.kersur.net> <x-ya02408000R0507972215140001@news.digex.net>
Organization: DEC
Reply-To: Everhart@Arisia.GCE.Com
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



The claim that something detects Digital microcode in an Alpha system is
complete rubbish. There's nothing like that anywhere. There are tables
in dkdriver that detect peculiarities of some drives, but dkdriver
will do its best to work with anything it doesn't recognize, and if
it follows the SCSI standard and can keep up with an Alpha, it will
generally work. I have a number of non-DEC SCSI disks on my VMS boxes
at home built by people like Toshiba and Seagate.

HOWEVER, be aware that the Digital disks do get tested so you can know
they DO work, and firmware bugs get fixed. Things that cause disks
to get stuck, hang, or worse. Remember that running in an environment
where a very fast processor is sending thousands of commands/sec to the
bus, where DMA is always done, where SCSI disconnect and reconnect are
expected, and where devices must decode LUNs as well as IDs, is very
different from a typical PC environment, where usually there is no DMA,
where disconnect and reconnect to the SCSI bus is not commonplace, and
where I/O is often synchronous instead of heavily asynchronous. Stuff that
works fine in the more relaxed PC kind of machine can readily fall over
on its side in a more demanding environment.

Testing in that kind of environment is not,unfortunately, cheap, but it
seems to be easier for some people to spread this misinformation about
DEC systems detecting DEC microcode than to just describe the limits of
what was tested. Perhaps they are resellers who don't know, but the
problems that people have come from the environment primarily, not from
systems code. I've worked on that code and have never seen any such
attempts to keep anything from running. 

However, what gets tested is the myriads of Digital branded disks, in house
at least, and if you want to try something else, it may or may not work
as you want. We try to make J Random device work, but the test requirements
are flat out too great to try EVERY disk someone comes up with and EVERY
release of firmware. 



