on it... ;)

>2. where does one get tapes for the tape drive. since i noticed there is
>no floppy drive, is the tape the way of getting info into the machine?

Do people still make TK50 tapes?
Dont use it more than you have to, the TK50 is one of the better
ideas and simultaneously one of the worst-designed ideas. 

>3. can someone describe the connectors on the back and the pinouts?

Im assuming you have a BA-23 cabinet and the 8-slot backplane. 
On the bulkhead you should see a 7-seg display and a 9 pin 
connector nearby, along with some dials and switches. 
These are the settings for the console, which btw is NOT standard serial. 
I dont rmember a  pinout off the top of my head,  but ask again when 
im near my docs.. The 9pin jack is the console line.  on the uVII, 
it runs from 300-19200 baud. 

>4. i presume it needs a terminal of some sort before i can even use it
>such as a vt220. can i use any ascii terminal or is a specific kind
>needed? 

you can use pretty much anything, but you need a nonstandard cable. 

>5. since i dont have a terminal yet, is there any way to tell its working
>ok without one? i have not powered the machine up yet. 
>any additional info or other locations of faq's would be appreciated. 


sure, you can connect a PC running an emulator to it, no need for a dedicated
terminal. 

>6. how does one *open* the machine? i know about the covers that pop off
>the front and back, but that's it. 

The mahcine itself is on a frame which slides out of the cabinet. 
(yup, you have a BA23 case)  There should be a couple screws and then
you heave the metal assembly out the back. It's heavy (you probably
figured that out already) and be prepared for a couple bumps on the ifngers
and an ouch or two. Watch out for those annoying  handles on the case, they
can cut. 

Anyways, once you have it open,  take note of the arrangement of boards:
the Q-bus  is very flexible as far as arrangement but organization has to be
in a serpentine pattern with termination at the ends. This is described in the 
microVAX faq, afaik. look there for more Qbus info. Most  options will
be self-terminating , so no worry there, but dont break the daisy-chain
style of the boards' insertion, unless you use some anti-temrination
boards to provide continuity to the next slot. 

On the far right you should see the console board, 
then the CPU board,
then the memory board. 
Then your usual disc controllers, serial controllers, tape controllers, 
and whatnot. 



Dont be afraid to play with it but put it all back together before you 
run it,  this isnt a PC that you can run open,  it will overheat!

oi. im feeling i left out some important stuff..

  ask again about that console line, i know i have the pinout 
around here somewhere...

