I used to own some Digital stock.  I bought it prior to the first release of 
the Alpha for around $60 per share.  I sold it at a considerable loss.

Digital's primary problem, in my opinion lies with the Board of Directors.
I would look at their picture and their brief biography in the annual report 
and myself "What special knowledge does this person have that contributes
to a leading technology firm".  Frankly the answer that I came up with for
most of its members was, "Very Little".  My impression is that the Board
is comprised of a bunch of old "Captains of Industry" who are on the board
because of the honor it bestows upon them.

What Digital needs is a Board comprised of younger energetic leaders that
are willing to ask lot's of questions and put Palmer's feet to the fire rather
than rubber stamp everything.

Throughout the 60's, 70's, and 80's Digital was an "Engineering" company.
Their products were well engineered and at that time, that was good enough.
Times have changed, and offering the best engineered products available
doesn't work in a commodity based industry.  Digital needs to decide what
kind of company they are.  I am waiting for some kind of cohesive message.

