In article <33BABCD3.3097@world.std.com>, Terry Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>How tempting. But such a presentation would force me to reveal that
>there's **NO** difference between Alpha chips for Unix, WNT, or OVMS.
>
>And DIGITAL wouldnt like that, now would they???

You mean they've sunk to the level of running the contact lens scam?  For
those of you not familiar with it, this is when a manufacturer sells "daily
use" and "extended use" versions of their contact lenses, with the former
considerably cheaper, since you throw them out every day.  Turns out that
for at least one company these were (and probably still are) the *exact*
same lenses.  The story ran on Dateline, or PrimeTime, or 20/20, I don't
recall which.  Gee, I wonder if the networks would be interested in this
story too, or at least Byte, InfoWeek, or Datamation?  Let's see, they
could title it "Desperate computer maker screws customers - again." 

Unfortunately, while there may not be any difference between the chips, 
Digital in its less than infinite wisdom, did not make these chips capable 
of fully supporting OpenVMS (and DU?) without external firmware, so it is 
possible to build a (slightly) cheaper machine that is WNT specific, and 
this prevents their WNT customers who purchase such machines from ever
converting them to run either Digital OS.  Brainy eh, design
your own hardware so as not to run the software that you alone can provide?
Leave it to Digital to come up with a new marketing concept - "building
yourself out of the marketplace." Digital used to advertise "Digital is a
software company", now they should advertise "Digital is an anti-software
company."  Hey, it won't sell any fewer computers than do the chimps! 

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 
