In article <01bc8885$6ce1da60$96c95882@aca.mcc.ac.uk>,
Tony Arnold <Tony.Arnold@mcc.ac.uk> wrote:

>However some of the third party suppliers are saying that they cannot
>guarantee their disks will work on  an Alphastation. They claim that there
>is somethinbg special in the microcode of a Digital SCSI disk that the
>system detrects and if it isn't their won't allow the disk to work.

This is highly unlikely, IMHO. Certainly under NetBSD I've used
all sorts of drives on my Alphas, with no problems whatsoever. I
can't imagine why it would be different for Digital Unix, VMS or NT.

If you want to test this, first, you could go out and buy a cheap
and slow 1 GB SCSI hard drive, plop it in, and see if it works. If
it does, no doubt the faster drives will work as well.

And every vendor, disk drive and machine alike, is going to claim
that he can't guarantee compatability with anyone else's hardware.
This is often not so much that they want you to keep buying their
own, more expensive hardware, as that they don't want to get into
a huge support problem when something weird happens on a configuration
they've not tested.

