In article <33B44420.42FA@world.std.com>, Terry Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>I have posted a writeup on last week's get-together in the Shannon Knows
>DEC portion of the Harvard Research Group website,
>
>http://www.hrgresearch.com
>
>Enjoy,
>

I did.  Well, as much as one can enjoy reading about such a painful topic.
It raised rather a lot more questions than it answered.

For instance, in another recent article about Providence Capital (get ye to
your search engines!) we find the following: "Herbert Denton, president of
Providence Capital, said he expected institutions representing more than 30
per cent of Digital's shares to attend the meeting tomorrow."  In the SKD
piece, we then learn "Denton brushed aside audience demands that Palmer be
deposed." Since the "audience" in this case apparently corresponds to 30%
of Digital's shares, and Denton controls "a small amount of Digital stock",
it would seem that he is in no position to "brush aside" anything. 

It would be interesting to see who actually owns Digital stock at this
point.  I mean, the stock has been a dog so long it has fleas, so what
sorts of big investors are still holding it?  I couldn't locate this
information in Digital's web site anywhere, but presumably it is public
record, at least for the very largest shareholders.  (Mr. Denton seems to
have access to it anyway.) 

Then there is the big question, if the stockholders, meaning in particular,
the institutional stockholders did clean house, who would they put in
charge of this shipwreck?  Maybe they could get Compaq to take it off their
hands, perhaps via a merger and a Digital managementectomy? 

Sigh.

In my book, the management of a company, and the CEO in particular, are
supposed to MANAGE the company.  That means make it work, not shuffle the 
chairs incessantly.  The board of the company is responsible for managing
the CEO.  In the case of Digital we have a situation where neither the
board nor the management is doing, by any conceivable measure, a good job,
and the stock holders are well within their rights to clean house.  Mr.
Palmer was named CEO in October 1992. Giving him a reasonable grace period
to get the job done, he should have long since turned the company around. 
It is patently obvious that he has not.  The board, if it was doing its
