Ulrich Ruede wrote:
> 
> What is technically the difference between a NT Alpha processor and
> a Unix Alpha?

They have different part numbers :-)
There are a couple of bits in the IDU CSR that control superpage
mapping, and these could potentially be hardwired to force NT mode on or
off. I don't think this has been done so far. (See the Hardware
Reference Manual for the 21164, Sectrion 2.1.1.4 and Table 5-5)

> And does this really result in a Digital workstation of
> the au series being downgradable to NT, but an a-series model not
> being upgradable to Unix (except Linux, of course)?
> 
Well, it certainly isn't supported to run Digital UNIX on a PWSa series.
And you would be violating your software licence.
There may also be problems with different revs of the motherboard or
firmware that make it fail.

> This seems to be an ugly old habit of Digital. I remember times when
> MicroVax workstations came with 8 extra serial ports but made
> unusable by smearing glue into the connectors. What's the electronic
> glue in the NT-version of the Alpha processor?
> 
Seems absurd, doesn't it? However, you can also look at it as a way to
release a low cost, low margin model while avoiding cannibalising the
higher margin market. But it certainly does piss off people who only
look at the cost of the hardware and consider that should set the
selling price of the system (the commodity model).

