jfmezei wrote:
> 
> mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu wrote:
> > In my book, the management of a company, and the CEO in particular, are
> > supposed to MANAGE the company.
> 
> I would add the word "LEAD" as well. A company without direction goes
> nowhere. And when the direction seems to constantly change or worse,
> kept secret, it doesn't help.
> 
> > Palmer was named CEO in October 1992. Giving him a reasonable grace period
> > to get the job done, he should have long since turned the company around.
> 
> It is my OPINION that Palmer has succeeded in cutting the fat. But he
> has failed in giving Digital a clear direction except as a Microsoft
> reseller.
> 
> Mr Palmer did a rather quick cleaning of the house early on, dropping
> products left and right. They dropped so many software packages that
> many have lost track of what Digital still has as "active" software
> packages.
> 

I would argue that DEC acted too slowly in downsizing its workforce. The
massive cuts of FY95 should have been implemented earlier on. Of course,
this was politically unfeasible until the April 15, 1994 "Ides of April"
$183.3M loss came along and bit DEC on the backside.

> Digital dropped their sales forces (with some twists and turns here and
> there) but does not seem to have succeeed in replacing it with effective
> sales mechanisms for the current "commodity" marketplace.

Agreed, but they **seem** to be doing better with channels these days. A
metric you might want to watch is percent direct/indirect revenue. When
Palmer took over, revenue was 25 indirect. By mid-94, indirect revenue
was 43 percent. That climbed to 58 percent by the end of FY95. DEC's
FY95 goal was 62 percent indirect; they have yet to achieve this figure.

> 
> The drop of VAX Document is one example of an inexcusable error. They
> dropped it because it was "legacy". It is only well after having dropped
> it that Palmer relented and re-instated because it took him that long to
> realise that it would cost way too much to convert all that
> documentation (something which differentiated Digital from the others in
> the past) to whatever new package Palmer has selected. This was the one
> event that made me question the planning and strategy behind all these
> cutbacks.

The planning and strategy boiled down to dollars and cents: if a product
isn't profitable, the only reason DEC might want to keep it around is to
protect the installed base. Apparently DEC had what it deemed an
insignificant VAX DOCUMENT I.B.--until the firm discovered that its own
tech writers were a significant base indeed!


Incidentally, some of the legacy stuff (DECdecision, DECwrite, etc) has
been taken over by Touch Technologies (http://www.ttisms.com)
> 
> Seems that Palmer was good at cutting stuff he didn't like, but not good
> at finding replacements.
> 
> Palmer worked hard to downsize Digital, but doesn't seem to have done
> much to increase its customer base and sales. As a matter of fact, he
> worked hard to alienate its loyal customer base (VMS).

Neither the customer base nor the sales have increased. I don't believe
that Palmer deliberately marginalized VMS. If in fact he did, the
acquisition of Wes Melling from Gartner Group offers evidence that
Palmer realized the error in his ways.

But somehow I think the ongoing operating system wars and political
correctness campaigns originate at a lower level than Bob's office.

> 
> The big cuts have been over for a while now, but we don't really see any
> rebuilding going on.

What was until the day before yesterday the PCBU is in investment mode.
And as anecdotal evidence, there have been plenty of DEC employment ads
in the Boston Globe lately, which represents a big change!

