In article <19970701191901.PAA11329@ladder02.news.aol.com>, suprdave@aol.com (SUPRDAVE) writes:
:i just picked up a microvax II...
:1. what is this box capable of?

  Running OpenVMS or ULTRIX/VAX.  Slowly -- this processor design is
  over a decade old, after all.  16 MB memory maximum.  Typically one
  or two RD54-series 159 MB disk drives were supported, assuming an
  RQDX3 controller is present.  Hooking a SCSI disk to this system
  involves use of a third-party controller, or a DEC KFQSA/HSDxx
  combination.

  For folks considering "home" systems, the VAXstation 3100 and/or the
  VAXstation 4000 series systems, or an AlphaStation system, would tend
  to make better home systems.  They're smaller, faster, more expandable,
  support graphics, and have native SCSI support.

:2. where does one get tapes for the tape drive. since i noticed there is
:no floppy drive, is the tape the way of getting info into the machine?

  The tape is likely a TK50 CompacTape.  Yes, this is the load, BACKUP,
  and interchange device.  CompacTape cassettes are available new and
  used from a variety of sources.

:3. can someone describe the connectors on the back and the pinouts?

:4. i presume it needs a terminal of some sort before i can even use it
:such as a vt220. can i use any ascii terminal or is a specific kind
:needed? 

  VT-compatible is prefered -- VT100, VT220, VT510, etc.

:5. since i dont have a terminal yet, is there any way to tell its working
:ok without one? i have not powered the machine up yet. 

  Yes.  The self-test count-down LED will get down to a value from 2
  to 0 if the hardware checks out.

:any additional info or other locations of faq's would be appreciated. 

  http://www.altavista.digital.com
  ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/vms
  http://www.dejanews.com/

  FAQs also usually get posted to comp.answers and news.answers,
     and usually also get collected at MIT's rtfm host...

:6. how does one *open* the machine? i know about the covers that pop off
:the front and back, but that's it. 

  Use a screwdriver -- on or near the back I/O panel will be a few screws
  at the top (BA23 "space heater") or at the side (BA123 "coffee table")
  of the I/O panel that will, when undone, allow access to the cabinet. 
  On the BA23, the I/O panel "drawbridge" drops down directly into the
  Q-bus.  On the BA123, the side cover pops off, and a second spring-clip
  cover is opened to allow access to the Q-bus.

  Now, when asking "what's in this box", you will find "Mnnn" numbers
  on the spines of the Q-bus cards.  Q-bus cards can be dual-width, or
  quad width -- dual cards fill half of the Q-bus slot, and quad cards
  fill the entire Q-bus slot.  We will need to know which box -- BA23
  or BA123, which Mnnn numbers are present, and which Mnnn numbers are
  in which Q-bus slots.  (There should be a sticker showing the slot
  number scheme -- the Q-bus slots are usually numbered from right to
  left, with the KA630 M7606 module normally in the right-most slot.)

  And DECUS members can acquire a "hobbyist" license for OpenVMS VAX.

