In article <5pcfpr$aas$1@was.hooked.net> sdyoung@well.com "Steven Young" writes:

[11/44 with hacks...]
>   Something I've noticed is that after the system has been on for a while,
> the fans speed up, most likely as a reaction to a thermostat or somesuch.
> What I'm wondering is, out of curiosity, is this standard PDP behaviour,
> or is it another hardware hack? 

It is _not_ (AFAIK) standard. I have the 11/44 PSU prints in front of me, and
the fan control logic is pretty simple. It's on the Bias/interface PCB (the
leftmost one in the PSU box).

E12 is a 555 timer which runs in astable mode. It clocks the 2 D-types in
E8 (7474). E4 (a 74S15) and E10d (74132) combine the outputs of these
flip-flops and drive the full-bridge driver Q16-Q21.

The only (electronic) way to vary the fan speed is to fiddle with E12. There
may have been some mods done here (With the board component-side up, and
connectors towards you, it's the rightmost 8 pin chip). But are you sure it's
not just old lubricant thinning out as the machine warms up?

You might hook a scope or counter to one of the fan leads (_neither_ are
grounded, and shorting either to ground will blow transistors. Don't ask...)
and see if the frequency changes as the machine warms up.

Take _great_ care if you open up the PSU. There's 400V at essentially unlimited
current on the 2 wires connectors on the top of some of the boards. This _will_
kill you. Pull the power plug and wait 5 minutes before removing the cover.


