In article <5pegae$d7k@corn.cso.niu.edu> system@niuhep.physics.niu.edu writes:

>I am need of confirmation (or denial :)  that I have stuck my foot in my mouth
>(again).  I expressed doubts that the DEC-10/DEC-20s were the equivalent to
>the mainframes of the day, esp. with respect to I/O.

I have to confirm your fears.  The disk drives in use on the DEC-20 (& DEC-10,
though I didn't use one of those) were the equivalents of IBM 3330-II and 3350
DASD, operating at the same transfer speeds as the Big Blue equipment.  The
memory capacity was in the same range as mid- to high-end 360/370 equipment,
and the PDP-10 boxes had virtual memory long before IBM "invented" it for the
370/158 and 370/168.

Large industrial and financial firms used them as their exclusive platform, so
I'd say the marketplace even viewed them as mainframes.
