Ulrich Ruede wrote:
> 
> I've just read on http://www.microway.com/mbpx.html this statement:
> 
> > Note: This note is in response to the issue on DEC UNIX vs NT/LINUX Alpha
> > pricing.  Digital has made a marketing decision to charge more for Alpha
> > UNIX processors. If you purchase an Alpha designated for NT and try to
> > load UNIX it will not run.  Internally the processor is different. The
> > additional cost, we believe, helps to pay for the 500 or so employees
> > at Digital who support UNIX development and maintenance. In addition,
> > Digital charges us for the SRM console plus license fees to run Digital
> > UNIX. We hope this clears up this confusing issue.
> 
> What is technically the difference between a NT Alpha processor and
> a Unix Alpha? And does this really result in a Digital workstation of
> the au series being downgradable to NT, but an a-series model not being
> upgradable to Unix (except Linux, of course)?
> 

I haven't investigated the source of the original statement about the
processors in the NT and UNIX Personal Workstations being different, but
IT IS ABSOLUTELY WRONG!!!! It is identical hardware, that is why DIGITAL
will sell a system with either NT or UNIX (and soon VMS), and be quite
happy for you to later change your mind and swap to the alternate O/S.
Dispite the marketing info being put out, this change can be made either
way i.e. if you don't find NT suits your needs, you can replace it with
DIGITAL UNIX.

There are only two CAVEATS involved:

1) different peripherals are qualified by different Operating Systems
i.e. NT systems support IDE Hard Drives but UNIX doesn't; or more
commonly, NT systems are sold with lower end graphics cards, which again
are not qualified/supported under DIGITAL UNIX. However this does NOT
apply to the motherboard, Alpha CPU, cache or memory. These ARE
IDENTICAL! So if you order a machine with peripherals qualified for both
O/S's, you can run either O/S, or even, as in my case, run both on the
same machine (as a Technical Support person in DIGITAL I need to be able
to dual boot my workstation into both UNIX and NT depending on what I
happen to be working on).

2) the second issue is the contentious one of pricing. I must be honest
and say that never have I heard such mis-information and plain wrong
statements (often supposedly by people who claim to know the real
reasons). The simple truth is that the price that DIGITAL charges for
the relevant software license (either NT or DIGITAL UNIX) to go with the
DPWS hardware, is driven to a very small extent by the higher
support/development costs associated with UNIX (as DIGITAL does the work
here rather than Microsoft),

BUT,

the major part of the license fee charged by DIGITAL for either MS NT or
DIGITAL UNIX is simply to cover the cost that DIGITAL has to pay to the
originaters of the software (i.e. to Microsoft for NT and to OSF [plus
other third parties for LSM etc] for DIGITAL UNIX). Thus the simple
reason a DIGITAL UNIX license costs more than an MS NT license is that
DIGITAL has to pay more to OSF et al. for each copy of UNIX than we do
for NT *END OF STORY*

There is no secret agenda to promote NT, there is no desire to charge
unsupportable prices for UNIX, it is simply the normal business of cost
of materials affects sale price.

Dave Bynion
DIGITAL Tech Support
Melbourne

(with all the usual caveats about personal opinions, personal
responsibility etc etc etc)



