In article <5p8iqs$lr0$2@sniff.shr.dec.com>, lederman@star.enet.dec.disable-autoreply.com (Bart Z. Lederman) writes:
> 
> Various people said one or more of the following:

<<< snipped useful comments about gratuitous use of HTML "features" >>>

>>Incidentally, would downloadable PDFs be of interest to anyone? I'm
>>trying to cost-justify the expense of Adobe Distiller.
> 
> Please don't. That is yet another step in the wrong direction: putting
> information in a proprietary format which requires additional software
> to interpret, and often cannot be read on-line.  I've run into this
> in quite a few places as well.  Once again, in most cases there is
> no information in the PDF file that couldn't be in more easily read
> text and image (JPG or GIF) files.  In one instance, I've seen
> a public transit time table which contained only colums of text
> (stations and times of departure) which was put into a PDF file.

While that example is one for which PDF might not be critical,
PDF _does_ allow the creator to maintain tight typographical
control over the output, and there are cases where that matters.

Many people encounter problems with PDF because they try to display
a PDF document (presuming they have display software) when that PDF
document was designed for the printed page.  HTML is designed to
adapt to whatever size and style of reading mechanism you choose,
and it does that by _not_ giving the creator tight control.

The best thing for readers is when a creator provides the same
material in multiple formats so the reader can choose.  Of course
that is not best for readers if it drives up prices of whatever the
creator is selling, or if it delays delivery of the information.

Larry Kilgallen



