> Yeah, and it needs a three phase supply (I think). I wouldn't
> recommend having one of these at home; thats assuming you could get it
> through the front door :-)

Nope, not a single PDP-11, or even the early vaxes (730-750-780)
actually required three phase power.  Some had plugs to go into
a three phase outlet but you'll find that they just plucked out
three 120V legs from it.  Armando Stetner managed to run a VAX
in a hotel room at a long ago UNIX conference by running extension
cords all over the place.  Some of the disk drives require either
208 or 240 though.

I think that a 750 just has a 30 A 120V plug on it.

780 CPU cabinets by the way, appear to have been designed to
be fork lifted.  They have small pads just inside the casters.
The lift driver that moved one of ours asked us not to tell him
how much it cost until he was done.  He was stunned to find
that he was forking a $300,000 (in those days) processor
around.



