In article <33B84A2D.41C6@meo.mts.dec.com>, Dave Bynion <dave.bynion@meo.mts.dec.com> writes:
>Ulrich Ruede wrote:
>> 
>> I've just read on http://www.microway.com/mbpx.html this statement:
>> 
>> What is technically the difference between a NT Alpha processor and
>> a Unix Alpha? And does this really result in a Digital workstation of
>> the au series being downgradable to NT, but an a-series model not being
>> upgradable to Unix (except Linux, of course)?
>> 
>
>I haven't investigated the source of the original statement about the
>processors in the NT and UNIX Personal Workstations being different, but
>IT IS ABSOLUTELY WRONG!!!! It is identical hardware, that is why DIGITAL
>will sell a system with either NT or UNIX (and soon VMS), and be quite
>happy for you to later change your mind and swap to the alternate O/S.

Wrong bucko, way, way wrong.  Get yourself a Multia and try to run OpenVMS
on it - it cannot be done.  OpenVMS on alpha demands more of the firmware
than does either WNT or DU.  (There is even one machine that was
apparently supposed to be able to run OpenVMS, but Digital screwed up: the
375 Mhz CPU module for the 2100.)  The reason for this, if memory serves, is
that OpenVMS depends on pieces outside of the Alpha chip itself to be sure
that certain commands complete without getting interrupted.  If they were
interrupted, then OpenVMS couldn't guarantee the state of the system/data,
and poof, you might as well be running Windows 95.  This raises two equally
interesting questions. 

1.  Why in the heck didn't Digital build the necessary locking instructions
into the alpha architecture directly?  Ok, so it wouldn't have been pretty,
but I cannot imagine that in the long run it wouldn't have been more cost
effective than messing about with N versions of the external firmware. 

2.  What do DU and WNT give up by NOT using these "guaranteed to complete"
instructions?  Off the top of my head I would guess that it means they 
cannot complete certain operations as quickly as can an OpenVMS system, 
since they must achieve the same operations using methods "further from the 
processor", if you will.

